    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3ed7c09519261311ee1a88a2d80403a19deba4b8e799c0bc6ee70ceb35fec79f",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source uses complementary base points rather than a uniformly best normalization: the base point arising directly from the standard product supports explicit positive-integer secondary-zeta values through Taylor-coefficient comparison and finite decompositions, while centering at the fixed point of the functional symmetry sacrifices an explicit normalization constant but makes meromorphic continuation and polar data substantially simpler. Expanding an arbitrary shift about that symmetric case transfers the continuation and terminating nonpositive-integer trace identities, yet the expansion is infinite at positive integers, so it does not erase the original base point's separate finite-value advantage.",
